## What Do You Actually Need for Fall Nail Art?

You do not need a forty-pound nail art kit to start. A dotting tool, a fine brush, and a bottle of striping tape cover almost every design in this post, and two of the three have free substitutes sitting in your bathroom cabinet right now.

Here's the honest toolkit for <strong>fall nail art</strong>. A dotting tool handles dots, leaves, and marbling drag-lines; a bobby pin or the rounded end of a hair grip does the identical job if you don't own one. A fine detail brush handles freehand lines and gold accents; a toothpick manages smaller work in a pinch, though the line will be less clean. Striping tape creates plaid's crisp edges, and there's genuinely no good substitute for this one, though it costs under three pounds at any pharmacy. A stamping plate and scraper are the only "must-buy" if you specifically want the stamped design in this post, but everything else here works without one.

Base coat, your polish colours, and top coat round it out. That's the full list. Nobody needs a professional kit to start <strong>fall nail art</strong> this weekend.

