## How to Prep Your Nails Before Any Design

Prep is where most beginner "mess-ups" actually begin, long before the design stage. Push cuticles back gently with an orange stick, buff the nail surface just enough to remove shine, and wipe each nail with a little acetone on a cotton pad to strip natural oils. Skipping this step is the single most common reason polish lifts within days.

Apply a thin base coat and let it dry completely; rushing this is the second most common mistake, and it's an invisible one until your marbling smears an hour later. Then apply your chosen base colour in two thin coats rather than one thick one. Thin coats dry properly between layers and give your design surface something stable to sit on, rather than something still soft underneath.

If your cuticle line wobbles, don't fix it yet. Let the base colour dry fully, then clean the edge with a small angled brush dipped in acetone. Precision at the cuticle line comes from cleanup, not from a steadier hand than you think you have.

