## Plaid Nail Art for Fall (Intermediate)

Everyone assumes plaid <strong>fall nail art</strong> needs a brush steady enough to paint a straight line freehand. It doesn't. Striping tape does that job for you, which is exactly why plaid belongs earlier in a beginner's skill arc than its finished look suggests.

Apply your base colour, a muted rust or forest green, and let it dry completely. Lay strips of striping tape vertically across the nail at even gaps, then paint horizontally over the whole nail in a contrasting colour, straight over the tape. Peel the tape away before the polish fully sets, revealing crisp vertical stripes underneath. Repeat the process running tape horizontally this time, painting thin vertical lines freehand or with a fine brush across the remaining gaps, and the crossed lines complete the plaid grid.

The tape is doing the precision work your hand doesn't have to. That's the whole secret behind plaid looking harder than it is.

Two colours is plenty for a first attempt. A third accent line in gold or cream, added last with a fine brush, gives it that finished, editorial plaid look without adding real difficulty.

