## Freehand Gold Accent Nails (Intermediate)

An accent nail carrying a single confident gold line does more for a fall manicure than a full hand of complicated detail. This is the technique where restraint outperforms effort.

Load a fine detail brush with gold polish or a metallic liner, and in one smooth motion, drag a single curved or diagonal line from one edge of the nail to the other. Don't go back over it. A second pass is what turns a clean line into a shaky one, because polish that's already begun to set drags under a brush instead of gliding. If the first attempt goes wrong, wipe it clean with an acetone-dipped brush immediately, while the base underneath is still protected by top coat, and try again.

Add gold leaf flakes at the base of the line with tweezers for texture, pressing gently before sealing with top coat. One accent nail per hand, painted last after every other nail is finished and cured, keeps the gold from smudging against your other fingers while you work.

