## What to Do When You Mess Up

Every mistake in <strong>fall nail art</strong> is fixable while the polish is still wet, which is most of the time you'll actually spend on this. Dip a small angled brush in acetone and clean up the edge, the smudge, or the overrun line directly, rather than removing the whole nail and starting again.

The fear of "ruining it" tends to come from working on your real hand first, with no room to practise. Buy a pack of practice nail tips, or simply paint a base coat onto a scrap of paper and treat it as a stand-in nail. Run through dotting, marbling, or a stamped design there first. Nothing is wasted, nothing is precious, and the muscle memory transfers directly to your actual nails afterwards.

If a whole nail genuinely goes wrong, acetone and a cotton pad remove it back to bare nail in under a minute. That's not failure, that's just the reset button every nail artist uses. Practice makes the technique automatic, not perfect. Perfect was never actually the target.

